== setup: unaccent and pg_trgm, two text search configurations, 200,000 generated rows per table, GIN indexes ==
loaded 200010 help articles and 200008 employers in 69669 ms; built 3 GIN indexes in 23228 ms
== demo: naive ILIKE, then tsvector + unaccent + websearch_to_tsquery + ts_rank_cd + ts_headline, pg_trgm for names, and the page ==
   200010 help articles (10 written in English and French, the rest a generated archive) and 200008 employers

## 1. The first version: ILIKE '%x%'
   concept: a substring match is accent-sensitive, typo-intolerant and unranked, and a leading % means no B-tree can help, so every row is read
   articles ILIKE '%elodie%': 0 rows
   ok   ILIKE misses the articles a member means by "elodie"
   articles ILIKE '%retraite anticipee%': 0 rows
   ok   ILIKE misses the articles a member means by "retraite anticipee"
   articles ILIKE '%Élodie%': 3 rows, in table order: early-retirement, transfer-out, adviser
   ok   only the exact accented spelling finds them, and the article about Élodie is not first
   employers ILIKE '%globx%': 0 rows
   ok   ILIKE finds no employer for the typo or missing accent "globx"
   employers ILIKE '%initek%': 0 rows
   ok   ILIKE finds no employer for the typo or missing accent "initek"
   employers ILIKE '%boulangerie elodie%': 0 rows
   ok   ILIKE finds no employer for the typo or missing accent "boulangerie elodie"

## 2. A text search configuration with unaccent
   concept: en_unaccent and fr_unaccent copy english and french, and run unaccent before the stemmer, so the document and the query are normalised the same way
   to_tsvector('french',      'Élodie, retraite anticipée') = 'anticip':3 'retrait':2 'élod':1
   to_tsvector('fr_unaccent', 'Élodie, retraite anticipée') = 'anticipe':3 'elod':1 'retrait':2
   to_tsvector('fr_unaccent', 'elodie retraite anticipee')  = 'anticipe':3 'elod':1 'retrait':2
   ok   with unaccent the accented text and the unaccented query give the same lexemes
   search "elodie": 6 hits, 3 English and 3 French
   ok   "elodie" finds the articles about Élodie in both languages
   search "retraite anticipee": fr [Retraite] [anticipée] : quand puis-je arrêter de travailler ?
   ok   "retraite anticipee" finds "Retraite anticipée"

## 3. websearch_to_tsquery
   concept: takes what people type (quotes for a phrase, - to exclude, OR) and never raises a syntax error, unlike to_tsquery
   "\"early retirement\" -tax" en: 'earli' <-> 'retir' & !'tax'       fr: 'early' <-> 'retir' & !'tax'
   "pension OR épargne"       en: 'pension' | 'epargn'               fr: 'pension' | 'epargn'
   "beneficiaries"            en: 'beneficiari'                      fr: 'beneficiar'
   "retraite & | ("           en: 'retrait'                          fr: 'retrait'
   to_tsquery('fr_unaccent', 'retraite & | (') -> syntax error in tsquery: "retraite & | ("
   ok   to_tsquery rejects raw user input that websearch_to_tsquery accepts
   early retirement: en [Early] [retirement]: when can I stop working?; en Ill-health [retirement]
   "early retirement" -tax: en [Early] [retirement]: when can I stop working?
   beneficiaries: en Naming a [beneficiary]
   ok   without quotes both words anywhere match 2 articles; the phrase keeps only the one where they are adjacent; the plural finds the singular title

## 4. Ranking: ts_rank_cd with weights
   concept: title words carry weight A and body words B; weights {0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 1.0} are {D, C, B, A}, so a title hit counts 2.5 times a body hit
   1.400  en  Meet [Élodie], your pensions adviser
   1.400  fr  [Élodie], votre conseillère retraite
   0.400  en  Early retirement: when can I stop working?
   0.400  en  Transferring your pension to another scheme
   0.400  fr  Retraite anticipée : quand puis-je arrêter de travailler ?
   0.400  fr  Transférer votre épargne vers un autre régime
   ok   the two articles with Élodie in the title rank above the four that only mention her in the body

## 5. Highlighting: ts_headline
   concept: returns the best fragments with the matched words marked; it normalises words with the same configuration, so the accented original is the one marked
   en: [Élodie] Durand answers members' questions by phone and video call, in English and in French. She can explain your options but cannot choose
   fr: [Élodie] Durand répond aux questions des adhérents par téléphone et en visioconférence, en français et en anglais. Elle explique vos options mais ne peut pas choisir à votre
   ok   ts_headline marks the original accented word for the unaccented query

## 6. Typos: pg_trgm on employer names
   concept: trigram similarity on f_unaccent(lower(name)) with a GIN gin_trgm_ops index; word_similarity (<%) compares the query with the best-matching part of a long name
   globx                Globex Corporation (0.67)
   initek               Initech (0.71)
   boulangerie elodie   Boulangerie Élodie (1), Boulangerie Evans Bellevue & Associés (0.72), Boulangerie Evans Bellevue & Fils (0.72)
   lefevre garage       Garage Lefèvre & Fils (1), Garage Legrand Bellevue Ltd (0.6), Garage Legrand Bellevue SA (0.6)
   ok   typos, missing accents and word order still find the right employer first
   the threshold trades recall for noise (pg_trgm.word_similarity_threshold, default 0.6):
     threshold 0.30: "boulanjerie elodi" matches 6583 employers
     threshold 0.45: "boulanjerie elodi" matches 106 employers
     threshold 0.60: "boulanjerie elodi" matches 1 employers
     threshold 0.80: "boulanjerie elodi" matches 0 employers
     at 0.60 the one match is Boulangerie Élodie (0.67)
   ok   a loose threshold floods the list, the default keeps the one meant, too strict a one loses it

## 7. Bad versus good on 200,000 rows each (EXPLAIN ANALYZE, best of 5)
   concept: the naive queries read every row (Seq Scan); the good ones go through the GIN indexes (Bitmap Index Scan)
   articles 'Élodie'
     bad  (ILIKE, exact spelling "Élodie"):  2344.7 ms, 3 rows, Seq Scan on help_articles
     good (tsvector, typed "elodie"):     0.4 ms, 6 rows, Bitmap Heap Scan on help_articles + Bitmap Index Scan on help_articles_search_en + Bitmap Index Scan on help_articles_search_fr + Index Scan on help_articles_pkey
   ok   articles 'Élodie': the bad query is a Seq Scan, the good one an index scan, at least 10 times faster
   articles 'retraite anticipee'
     bad  (ILIKE, exact spelling "retraite anticipée"):  5828.6 ms, 1 rows, Seq Scan on help_articles
     good (tsvector, typed "retraite anticipee"):     0.4 ms, 1 rows, Bitmap Heap Scan on help_articles + Bitmap Index Scan on help_articles_search_en + Bitmap Index Scan on help_articles_search_fr + Index Scan on help_articles_pkey
   ok   articles 'retraite anticipee': the bad query is a Seq Scan, the good one an index scan, at least 10 times faster
   employers 'initek'
     bad  (ILIKE, exact spelling "Initech"):   272.6 ms, 1 rows, Seq Scan on employers
     good (trigram, typed "initek"):     0.2 ms, 1 rows, Bitmap Heap Scan on employers + Bitmap Index Scan on employers_name_trgm
   ok   employers 'initek': the bad query is a Seq Scan, the good one an index scan, at least 10 times faster

## 8. The search page
   concept: the page escapes the article text, then turns ts_headline's markers into <mark>
[search] help centre on :53057
   /search?q=elodie: 4 highlights in English results, 4 in French, first employer: Boulangerie Élodie similarity 1
   ok   the page shows highlighted results in both languages and the accented employer
   /search?q="><img src=x onerror=alert(1)> elodie: 0 <img> elements in the page, search box shows "\"><img src=x onerror=alert(1)> elodie"
   ok   markup typed in the query is shown as text, never rendered

